From 553faa42cb73fcf574610d4081430e2fc957cc33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 23:35:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [WIP][Qwen4Exp] Give QSA an INT8 per-token-head KV cache

Serves the main attention cache as int8 with one fp32 scale per (token, KV head)
per side, reusing the mode that already exists in the tree rather than inventing
one: CacheDType "int8_per_token_head", KVQuantMode.INT8_PER_TOKEN_HEAD, and the
store kernel triton_reshape_and_cache_flash_per_token_head_quant.

Scales ride in inline cell padding, the way TritonAttentionBackend does it, and
NOT in a companion tensor. Prefix-cache block hashes are group-scoped, so data
and scales in different groups could hit or evict independently and pair a
block of data with an unrelated block of scales, which is silent numerical
corruption rather than a failure.

Read path deliberately unimplemented: qsa_sparse_paged_attention raises for this
mode and the warmup raises too, so an int8 boot fails loudly at startup instead
of computing wrong numbers. The fused qsa_prepare writer is refused for this
mode on every capability, not only below sm_89, because it derives the head
width from shape[-1] // 2 and has no output for a per-token scale.

PARKED, not for promotion. Its reason for existing was a measured 3.1x attention
speedup in a third-party int8 kernel. That measurement timed a Python wrapper
eagerly and was wrong; under CUDA graph replay our own kernel is 1.54x faster
than theirs, and the whole sparse read is 1.3% of the decode step. What is left
of the motivation is accuracy: this FP8 checkpoint ships no KV scale tensors and
QSA only registers default 1.0 scales, so the served e4m3 cache is uncalibrated.

Co-authored-by: Maki
